## Page 17

We need to handle overflow and underflow.

Product of many terms or factorials.

1. Underflow example: $(10^{-1})^{1000} = 10^{-1000}$ 

   
2. Overflow example: $1000! \approx 10^{3000}$ 

## Page 18

Representing numbers in logarithm form:

$$
l_x = \log(x)
$$

This approach avoids overflow when computing very large or very small exponential terms like $e^{2000}$ or $e^{-2000}$. Code might use a `return log(...)` statement.

## Page 19

Computing with Logarithms.

Multiplication becomes addition:

$$
\log(e^{l_1} \cdot e^{-l_2}) = l_1 - l_2
$$

$$
\log \left( \prod_{i=1}^{N} p_i \right) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} l_i
$$

Division becomes subtraction:

$$
\log(x_1 / x_2) = l_1 - l_2
$$

Example: $x_1 = 2^{2000}$, $x_2 = 2^{2201}$.

## Page 20

Computing Log-Sum-Exp.

$$
\log( \sum_{i=1}^n x_i ) = \log( \sum_{i=1}^n e^{l_i} )
$$

To avoid overflow, extract the maximum value:

$$
\log( \sum_{i=1}^n e^{l_i - m} \cdot e^m ) = \log( \sum_{i=1}^n e^{l_i - m} ) + m
$$

## Page 21

Implementation of the Log-Sum-Exp trick.

1. Let $m = \max(l_i)$ 

   
2. Compute $\log( \sum e^{l_i - m} ) + m$ 

This prevents computing huge exponents like $e^{2001}$ directly which yields $Inf$. By shifting by $m$, the largest exponent evaluated is safely offset (e.g. $e^0 = 1$).

## Page 22

Computing $\log(x - y)$ when $x > y$:

$$
\log(x - y) = \log(e^{l_x} - e^{l_y})
$$

Factor out $e^{l_x}$:

$$
\log(x - y) = \log(e^{l_x} (e^{l_x - l_y} - e^0)) = l_x + \log(1 - e^{l_y - l_x})
$$

